+# Windows 11 ARM64 verification — 2026-10-26
+
+The ARM64 half of the verification feature for this repository's CLI: install
+`graphhelm` through the documented build-from-source path on a genuine Windows 11 ARM64
+(Snapdragon) host and reach a clean `graphhelm --version` from a native ARM64 binary
+(exit code 0, exactly one line shaped `graphhelm <version>`, nothing on stderr). This
+attempt could not run its main observer: **no genuine Windows 11 ARM64 device was
+available at verification time**, so the record below closes as
+**`OBSERVER_MISSING`** rather than claiming the platform. The x86-64 authoring host is
+documented with its full redundant signal set and an explicit negative-control run, so a
+later reader can see why that outcome is not an ARM64 verification and what completes it.
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Commit under test | `708cd9075ef496d9c6add3ebdcf9055956928e10` (branch `main`; HEAD of this checkout at session start) — no binary was built against it on this host; the executed binary is a preinstalled preview (named in the negative-control block) |
+| Host | Gigabyte X670 AORUS ELITE AX desktop, Microsoft Windows 11 Pro, build `10.0.26200`, AMD Ryzen 9 9950X, **x86-64 by every signal in the host-identity block below** — not a Windows-on-ARM device |
+| Source | this task checkout at the commit above (branch `b`: no device to clone onto; no clone step was run) |
+| Toolchain | default host triple `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`; cargo 1.97.1 (c980f4866 2026-06-30), active via the checkout's `rust-toolchain.toml`; installed targets `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`, `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` (no `aarch64*`); VS 2022 BuildTools MSVC 14.44.35207 with host toolsets `Hostx64` / `Hostx86` only |
+| Started / ended | `2026-10-26T22:42:26Z` / `2026-10-26T22:48:33Z` (UTC) |
+| Result | **`OBSERVER_MISSING`** — no genuine Windows 11 ARM64 (Snapdragon) device available; the ARM64 runtime promise remains unobserved and the platform stays unverified in the guide's wording |
+| Secrets | none involved in this run; the sweep at the end confirms the transcript carries no 64-hex string |
+| Cost | none — no paid infrastructure |
+
+## What was verified, and what was not
+
+**Verified on this host:**
+
+- The host's x86-64 identity, from the redundant signal set below (agreement of the set,
+  not any single query).
+- The negative control of the version contract on the preinstalled x86-64 preview
+  binary: exit code 0, exactly one stdout line shaped `graphhelm <version>`, empty stderr —
+  and the installed image's PE COFF machine reads `0x8664` (x86-64).
+- That the documented prerequisites check for the Windows build path
+  (`vswhere -requires Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.x86.x64`) answers as
+  written on this machine.
+
+**Not verified (and therefore still unclaimed):**
+
+- The ARM64 runtime promise — install + native `--version` on Windows 11 ARM64 — has no
+  observation here. Reasons this machine cannot serve, each measured rather than assumed:
+  1. x86-64 by all four signals (`PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE=AMD64`, `SystemType=x64-based PC`,
+     CPU model AMD Ryzen 9 9950X, rustup host triple `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`);
+  2. no `aarch64*` Rust target is installed, so no ARM64 toolchain half exists locally;
+  3. the VS BuildTools MSVC 14.44.35207 installation carries only `Hostx64` / `Hostx86`
+     toolsets — no ARM64 host linker;
+  4. Windows 11 Pro on x86-64 does not emulate ARM64 at all, so no ARM64 image can even
+     execute on this machine — an emulator or a cross-build would be a proxy, and this
+     repository does not count a proxy as proof of the ARM64 promise.
+- A clean x86-64 result (measured below) explicitly **does not satisfy** the ARM64
+  confirmation; it is recorded so a later reader cannot mistake it for one.
+
+**Supersession.** When a genuine device runs the sequence and passes, that dated
+transcript becomes the authoritative record for (Windows 11, ARM64), links back to this
+one, and flips the guide's wording in the same follow-up change. This record is never
+rewritten; supersession replaces authority, not content.
+
+## Host identity (the redundant signal set, verbatim)
+
+All four readings, taken before anything else was run. Agreement of the set is what
+counts: a lone WMI architecture code has already been measured to mislead on exactly this
+machine (it reports code 9 — the ARM64 code — while every other signal says x86-64), so
+it is included for completeness and treated as noise.
+
+```
+PS> $env:PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE
+AMD64
+
+PS> (Get-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem).SystemType
+x64-based PC
+
+PS> Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Name
+AMD Ryzen 9 9950X 16-Core Processor
+
+PS> (Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor).Architecture
+9   # the ARM64 code, contradicting every other signal above — recorded, not believed
+
+PS> rustup show
+Default host: x86_64-pc-windows-msvc
+```
+
+Expected for a genuine ARM64 host would have been `ARM64` / `ARM64-based PC` / a
+Snapdragon part / `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc`. Any one x86-64 reading disqualifies the
+device; this host is x86-64 by all four.
+
+## Negative control: the version sequence on the x86-64 host (not an ARM64 verification)
+
+The contract command, captured verbatim with its exit code:
+
+```
+PS> graphhelm --version; Write-Output "exit=$LASTEXITCODE"
+graphhelm 0.1.1
+exit=0
+```
+
+Stderr was redirected to a file and measured: 0 bytes. The printed number is **recorded,
+never asserted**: it comes from a preinstalled preview binary resolved from PATH
+(`%USERPROFILE%\.cargo\bin\graphhelm.exe`), not from a build of the commit under test —
+which is precisely why acceptance is on the shape, not a pinned digit.
+
+The executed image's own architecture, read from the PE header:
+
+```
+PS> $exe = (Get-Command graphhelm).Source
+PS> $b = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllBytes($exe)
+PS> [BitConverter]::ToUInt16($b, [BitConverter]::ToInt32($b, 60) + 4)
+34404   # decimal — 0x8664, an x86-64 image
+```
+
+`0xAA64` would have been required for the ARM64 claim; `0x8664` fails it even though the
+version line was clean.
+
+## Toolchain state (why no local ARM64 route exists either)
+
+```
+PS> cargo +1.97.1 --version
+cargo 1.97.1 (c980f4866 2026-06-30)
+
+PS> rustup target list --installed
+x86_64-pc-windows-msvc
+x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
+
+# C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\2022\BuildTools\VC\Tools\MSVC\14.44.35207\bin
+# contains exactly: Hostx64, Hostx86   (no ARM64 host toolset)
+
+PS> & 'C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\Installer\vswhere.exe' -latest -products * -requires Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.x86.x64 -property installationPath
+C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\2022\BuildTools
+```
+
+A cross-compile from here to `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` and copying the `.exe` over would
+need the ARM64 target plus an ARM64 link toolchain (neither installed) and would still
+leave the *running* proof to a device — a proxy at both the build and the run step.
+
+## How this verification completes
+
+On a genuine Windows 11 ARM64 (Snapdragon) laptop: establish the host identity with the
+same four readings before installing anything, install the documented prerequisites
+(Visual Studio Build Tools 2022 with the "Desktop development with C++" workload for the
+native ARM64 MSVC linker, rustup with the pinned 1.97.1 toolchain — rustup defaults to
+`aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` there), build exactly as the guide says
+(`cargo +1.97.1 install --locked --path apps/cli`), run `graphhelm --version`, read the
+installed image's PE machine expecting `0xAA64`, and write a dated transcript in this
+format with Result **pass**. All of those steps are documented in
+[`docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`](../install/GETTING_STARTED.md) §1, including the
+platform status of this record until the pass lands.
+
+## Sweep
+
+```
+PS> Select-String -Path .\docs\acceptance\windows-arm64-2026-10-26.md -Pattern '[0-9a-f]{64}'
+(no matches)
+```
+
+No token or key appears anywhere in this record; the sweep above found no 64-hex string
+in the finished file.
