+
+## Notes
+
+- **Iteration 1 — all items pass.** The spec was reviewed line by line against each item; no revision iterations were required.
+- **[NEEDS CLARIFICATION] policy**: zero markers were left. This run is unattended with a directive to make reasonable assumptions and continue, so every open decision was resolved and recorded in `Assumptions` rather than posed as a question:
+  - *Verified install path for ARM64* → build-from-source (the only path the current release offers for non-x86-64 hosts); a prebuilt ARM64 archive is explicitly out of scope. See Assumptions ("Install path is build-from-source") and Out of Scope.
+  - *Definition of "verified"* → adopted the repository's existing standard (commands actually executed on-platform, transcript retained), consistent with how the guide evidences x86-64/Ubuntu. See Assumptions.
+- **"No implementation details" boundary**: because this feature verifies a developer-facing CLI, its acceptance criteria are inherently about *observable contract* — exit code 0, one-line `graphhelm <version>` output, and that the produced binary is native to an ARM64 host (FR-001..FR-003). Those describe *what must be true when observed*, not how the tool is implemented. Low-level observer hints (Rust host triple `aarch64`/PE machine, WMI/OS fields) appear only in Edge Cases and Assumptions as evidence for distinguishing a genuine ARM64 host from an emulated one — not as implementation prescriptions for the change itself.
+- **Success criteria are technology-agnostic**: expressed as user/business outcomes (single guided pass, 100% success, 100% reader awareness, 0 documentation contradictions, reproducible transcript), not internal metrics.
+- **Observation recorded, not over-claimed**: on the authoring host (Windows 11 Pro build 26200, x86-64 AMD Ryzen 9950X) `graphhelm --version` was observed to exit 0 and print `graphhelm 0.1.1`, but the installed binary is x86-64 (PE machine `0x8664`). This is captured in Assumptions so planning/implementation targets a *genuine* Windows 11 ARM64 device and does not mistake an x86-64 run for the ARM64 confirmation (FR-007). If no such device is available at verification time, the outcome is a recorded missing observer, not a "verified" claim.
