+# Data Model: Windows 11 ARM64 CLI Install Verification
+
+**Feature**: [spec.md](spec.md) · **Date**: 2026-10-26 · **Stage**: Phase 1 (`speckit.plan`)
+
+The spec names two key entities — *Verified Platform Record* and *Verification Evidence*.
+This feature persists them as Markdown documentation (no database, no schema change), so
+the model below fixes their fields, validation rules, and state transitions precisely
+enough that a later reader can audit a "verified" claim and reproduce it (FR-006, SC-005).
+
+## Entity 1: Verified Platform Record
+
+A row in the guide's platform/verification statement. One record per (OS, architecture)
+pair; for this feature the record of interest is
+`(Windows 11, ARM64, build_from_source)`.
+
+| Field | Type / domain | Required | Rule |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `platform_os` | string, e.g. `Windows 11` | yes | Must name OS + edition class actually verified (a Windows 11 ARM64 result does not cover Windows 10) |
+| `platform_arch` | `ARM64` \| `x86-64` | yes | The CPU architecture the host genuinely runs |
+| `install_method` | `prebuilt_download` \| `build_from_source` | yes | The method actually executed, not a possible one |
+| `status` | `verified` \| `observer_missing` (see transitions) | yes | Only `verified` may appear in the guide's verified-platforms list |
+| `evidence_path` | repository-relative path to a transcript under `docs/acceptance/` | yes when `status` is `verified` or `observer_missing` | Must exist in the same PR as the status it supports |
+| `commit_under_test` | full git SHA of the tree the binary was built from | yes | Recorded inside the transcript's header table |
+| `recorded_on` | ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD) | yes | Date of the run |
+| `superseded_by` | path of a newer transcript, or empty | optional | Set only when a newer run replaces the current one; old transcripts are never rewritten |
+
+### Validation rules
+
+1. `status = verified` ⇒ the referenced transcript has Result **pass**, its host signals
+   agree on `platform_arch`, and its binary PE machine matches
+   (`0xAA64` for ARM64, `0x8664` for x86-64).
+2. `install_method = prebuilt_download` ⇒ the release listing
+   (`docs/releases/<version>.md`) contains an archive for exactly that OS + arch
+   (FR-005). For this feature `install_method` is `build_from_source`; no ARM64 archive
+   exists in the listing, so no prebuilt claim may be written.
+3. Guide consistency invariant (SC-004): the set of platforms the guide lists as
+   **verified** equals the set of records with `status = verified`. Any platform absent
+   from that set must not be worded as verified, however informally (FR-007).
+4. English-only text (repository documentation rule).
+
+## Entity 2: Verification Evidence (transcript)
+
+A file `docs/acceptance/windows-arm64-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`, format fixed by the existing
+clean-host records (research R4). Its observable content contract:
+
+### Header table (fixed rows)
+
+| Row | Content rule |
+|---|---|
+| Commit under test | full SHA of the checkout the binary was built from |
+| Host | device/model + OS name/build + architecture, stated from the redundant signal set below |
+| Source | how the source tree arrived at the host (e.g. `git clone` URL + SHA) |
+| Toolchain | rustup default host triple (expect `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc`) + Rust version 1.97.1 + MSVC toolset version present |
+| Started / ended | UTC timestamps from the transcript itself |
+| Result | `pass` \| `fail` \| `OBSERVER_MISSING` (branch (b): which observer was absent) |
+| Secrets | tokens/keys as SHA-256 digests only, plus a note of the sweep performed |
+| Cost | expected: none (no paid infrastructure) |
+
+### Host-identity block (redundant signals — research R3)
+
+All four are captured, and agreement of the set is what counts:
+
+| Signal | Command / query | Expectation for a genuine ARM64 host |
+|---|---|---|
+| Process environment | `$env:PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE` | `ARM64` |
+| System type | `Get-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem` → `SystemType` | `ARM64-based PC` |
+| Toolchain triple | `rustup show` → default host | `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` |
+| CPU model | `Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor` → `Name` | an ARM/Snapdragon part (and *not* treated as decisive alone: the authoring host returned architecture code 9 while being x86-64 by every other signal) |
+
+### Binary-identity block
+
+| Signal | Command / method | Expectation |
+|---|---|---|
+| PE COFF machine of the installed `graphhelm.exe` | read bytes at offset `0x3C` + 4 (as done on the authoring host, where this yielded `0x8664`) | `0xAA64` for ARM64 |
+
+### Version-run block
+
+The verbatim command and output of `graphhelm --version`: exit code `0`, exactly one
+stdout line matching `graphhelm <version>` (semver-shaped), nothing on stderr — per
+[contracts/version-command.md](contracts/version-command.md). The exact printed number is
+recorded but never asserted (research R5).
+
+### Reproduction requirements
+
+- The "what was verified, and what was not" section states the limits honestly
+  (mirrors `install-rehearsal-2026-09-13.md`).
+- A maintainer re-running the sequence on an equivalent ARM64 host must reproduce
+  Result `pass` with 0 unexpected failures (SC-005); the version number may differ per
+  R5.
+
+## State transitions
+
+States: `unverified` (no run yet) → terminal-ish outcomes recorded per run. Transitions
+are caused only by runs; nothing flips a status without a transcript.
+
+```text
+                          run lands: transcript Result = pass, signals agree
+        unverified ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────► verified
+             ▲                                                                      │
+             │  no ARM64 device available:                                          │ dependencies/toolchain changed;
+             │  branch (b) record names the missing observer                        │ re-run cannot complete or fails
+             ▼                                                                      ▼
+       observer_missing ◄─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── observer_missing
+             │
+             │ next run passes (device becomes available)
+             └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────► verified
+```
+
+Rules:
+
+- **`unverified → verified`**: requires a pass transcript satisfying validation rule 1;
+  the guide edit and the transcript land in the same change (one review, one merge).
+- **`* → observer_missing`**: whenever a re-run is blocked by an absent device or fails
+  on the host, a new dated transcript records why and becomes the authoritative record
+  (`superseded_by` links forward on any later replacement). Earlier transcripts are
+  never edited after the fact — consistent with the repository's append-only evidence
+  culture; supersession replaces *authority*, not *content*.
+- **`observer_missing → verified`**: the normal follow-up once a device is available
+  (branch (a) runs after branch (b) has already landed).
+- At any instant, exactly one record per (OS, arch) pair is authoritative — the newest
+  un-superseded transcript.
+
+## Relationships
+
+```text
+Verified Platform Record (Windows 11, ARM64)
+   │  1 : 1 (current), 1 : n (history via superseded_by)
+   ▼
+Verification Evidence (docs/acceptance/windows-arm64-<date>.md)
+   ▲
+   │ linked from (cited, like the two existing clean-host records)
+docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md  ──── consistency-checked against ──── docs/releases/0.1.0.md
+```
+
+The document-level invariants these pointers enforce are written out in
