+# Quickstart: validating the Windows 11 ARM64 CLI install verification
+
+**Feature**: [spec.md](spec.md) · **Date**: 2026-10-26 · **Stage**: Phase 1 (`speckit.plan`)
+
+Runnable validation scenarios for this feature. Each scenario names its observer, its
+commands, and the exact expected outcome. Contracts live in
+[contracts/](contracts/) and the evidence format in [data-model.md](data-model.md); this
+guide does not restate them.
+
+> **Read first — which branch applies.** The core promise is observable **only on a
+> genuine Windows 11 ARM64 device**. As of 2026-10-26 the authoring machine is x86-64
+> (research R6) and no ARM64 host is reachable from it. If a Snapdragon laptop is at hand
+> when the verification runs, do **Scenario 1**. If not, follow the **Missing-observer
+> path** and report `OBSERVER_MISSING` — do not mark the platform verified on the
+> strength of any other machine (FR-007).
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+| For | Needed | How to check |
+|---|---|---|
+| Scenario 1 | A Windows 11 ARM64 (Snapdragon) laptop, clean or with the prerequisites absent | Scenario 1 step 1 establishes this |
+| Scenario 1 | Visual Studio Build Tools 2022 with the "Desktop development with C++" workload (provides the native ARM64 MSVC linker), Rust via rustup, git | guide's prerequisites + research R2 |
+| Scenario 2 | Any x86-64 Windows host (the authoring machine qualifies) with the same toolchain shape | `PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE` = `AMD64` |
+| Scenario 3 | The repository checkout itself (no device needed) | `git status` |
+
+Network is needed for clone and winget/rustup fetches; everything after the build runs
+offline.
+
+## Scenario 1 — Install and verify on the ARM64 host (the actual feature)
+
+Observer: `graphhelm --version` executing natively on ARM64 hardware, plus the binary's
+own image type. Covers FR-001…FR-003, FR-006, SC-001…SC-002.
+
+1. **Establish the host identity first** (all four readings, before installing anything):
+
+   ```powershell
+   $env:PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE                      # expect: ARM64
+   (Get-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem).SystemType   # expect: ARM64-based PC
+   Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Name   # expect: a Snapdragon/ARM part
+   ```
+
+   If any of these says x86-64, this is not the device — stop and use the missing-observer
+   path (or another machine). Record the readings; they go into the transcript's
+   host-identity block.
+
+2. **Install the documented prerequisites** (guide §Prerequisites, plus the ARM64 linker):
+
+   ```powershell
+   winget install --id Microsoft.VisualStudio.2022.BuildTools --exact --source winget --accept-source-agreements --accept-package-agreements
+   # then add the "Desktop development with C++" workload to that installation (ARM64 toolset)
+   winget install --id Rustlang.Rustup --exact --source winget --accept-source-agreements --accept-package-agreements
+   $env:Path = "$env:USERPROFILE\.cargo\bin;$env:Path"
+   rustup toolchain install 1.97.1 --profile minimal --component rustfmt clippy
+   rustup show                                     # expect default host triple: aarch64-pc-windows-msvc
+   ```
+
+   Any failure here is a finding for the record, not a detour to improvise past silently
+   (SC-001).
+
+3. **Build from source exactly as the guide says** (guide §1):
+
+   ```powershell
+   git clone https://github.com/stabem/GraphHelm.git
+   cd GraphHelm
+   cargo +1.97.1 install --locked --path apps/cli
+   ```
+
+   Record the commit SHA the tree is at (`git rev-parse HEAD`) — it becomes the
+   transcript's "Commit under test".
+
+4. **Run the contract command** and capture verbatim output plus exit code:
+
+   ```powershell
+   graphhelm --version; Write-Output "exit=$LASTEXITCODE"
+   ```
+
+   Expect per [contracts/version-command.md](contracts/version-command.md): exit 0, one
+   line `graphhelm <version>`, empty stderr. Note the printed version — recorded, not
+   asserted (research R5).
+
+5. **Prove the binary is native**, not emulated:
+
+   ```powershell
+   $exe = (Get-Command graphhelm).Source
+   $b = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllBytes($exe)
+   [BitConverter]::ToUInt16($b, [BitConverter]::ToInt32($b, 60) + 4)   # expect 0xAA64 (42628)
+   ```
+
+   `0xAA64` passes FR-003; anything else fails even if step 4 was clean.
+
+6. **Write the transcript** `docs/acceptance/windows-arm64-<date>.md` exactly per the
+   data model (header table with started/ended UTC times, result, secrets row, cost; "what
+   was verified, and what was not"; the verbatim blocks above) and **update the guide** in
+   the same change: verified-platforms statement naming Windows 11 ARM64 as verified via
+   build-from-source with the steps and a link to the new record
+   ([contracts/verified-platform-docs.md](contracts/verified-platform-docs.md)).
+
+## Scenario 2 — Negative control on the x86-64 authoring host
+
+Observer: the same sequence demonstrating it is *not* the ARM64 confirmation. Covers User
+Story 1 scenario 3; guards against the exact mistake already measured on this machine
+(WMI architecture code 9 on an x86-64 CPU — research R3).
+
+1. Run steps 1, 4, 5 of Scenario 1 on the x86-64 host.
+2. Expect: `PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE=AMD64`, `SystemType=x64-based PC`, PE machine `0x8664`
+   (measured today on the installed preview binary: it prints `graphhelm 0.1.1`, exit 0).
+3. State the result explicitly in the record: **"not an ARM64 verification."**
+
+## Scenario 3 — Documentation consistency (no device needed)
+
+Observer: text-level agreement between the guide and the release listing. Covers FR-005,
+SC-004, D2/D4 of the docs contract.
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```powershell
+# the guide must not claim any ARM64 prebuilt download
+Select-String -Path docs\install\GETTING_STARTED.md -Pattern 'arm' -SimpleMatch:$false
+# the release listing must still show only its real archives
+Get-Content docs\releases\0.1.0.md
+# every "verified" platform named in the guide must resolve to a cited transcript under docs/acceptance/
+Select-String -Path docs\install\GETTING_STARTED.md -Pattern 'verified','acceptance'
+```
+
+Expected: no sentence anywhere asserts a downloadable ARM64 archive; each verified
+platform statement cites a `docs/acceptance/` record; all text is English. Re-run these
+checks after *every* documentation edit in the change.
+
+## Missing-observer path (branch (b))
+
+When Scenario 1 cannot run because no genuine ARM64 device is available:
+
+1. Land the guide with the ARM64 build-from-source steps and an honest status line — the
+   platform is **not** represented as verified — pointing at the record below (FR-007, SC-003's steps half).
+2. Still write `docs/acceptance/windows-arm64-<date>.md`: header table with Result
+   `OBSERVER_MISSING`, the host-identity block documenting *why this machine cannot serve*
+   (it is x86-64 by the four redundant signals), and the Scenario 2 negative-control block.
+3. Report completion ending with `OBSERVER_MISSING:` naming the absent device. When a
+   device later becomes available, run Scenario 1, link the pass record as the
+   supersedure, and flip the guide's wording in that follow-up change (data-model state
+   transition `observer_missing → verified`).
+
+## Outcome map
+
+| Scenario | Proves | Spec anchors |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | install + native run + retained evidence | FR-001…FR-004, FR-006; SC-001, SC-002, SC-005 |
+| 2 | x86-64 result is not ARM64 proof | US1 scenario 3; edge cases |
+| 3 | guide ↔ release listing agree; English-only | FR-004, FR-005, FR-007; SC-003, SC-004 |
+| Missing-observer | blocked ≠ assumed | FR-007; US3 scenario 2; JPD rules |
