+## R1 — Verified install route on Windows 11 ARM64
+
+- **Decision**: Build from source, natively on the ARM64 device, through the guide's
+  existing section 1 route (`git clone` → `cargo +1.97.1 install --locked --path apps/cli`).
+  Neither the plan nor the documentation may introduce or imply a prebuilt ARM64 archive.
+- **Rationale**: `docs/releases/0.1.0.md` publishes exactly two archives —
+  `graphhelm-v0.1.0-windows-x86_64.zip` and `graphhelm-v0.1.0-linux-x86_64.tar.gz`, plus
+  `SHA256SUMS.txt` — and already says "For macOS, ARM, and other hosts, [build from
+  source]." The spec puts publishing an ARM64 archive out of scope, and FR-005 forbids
+  claiming one. Build-from-source is therefore simultaneously the only available route
+  and the spec-assumed one.
+- **Alternatives considered**:
+  - *Publish a prebuilt `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` archive* — rejected: packaging,
+    checksums, and the download listing are explicitly out of scope; would also turn a
+    documentation feature into a release feature.
+  - *Cross-compile from the x86-64 authoring host and copy the `.exe` to the device* —
+    rejected: the host is not configured for it (R8) and the copy step is an
+    undocumented detour against SC-001's "zero undocumented detours."
+
+## R2 — Build toolchain a fresh ARM64 laptop actually needs
+
+- **Decision**: The ARM64 steps in the guide must state the MSVC C++ build toolset
+  explicitly (Visual Studio Build Tools 2022, "Desktop development with C++", which
+  provides the native ARM64 MSVC linker), alongside the guide's existing Rust 1.97.1 /
+  git / PowerShell prerequisites. On an ARM64 Windows host, rustup's default target is
+  `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc`, so no `rustup target add` step is required.
+- **Rationale**: Rust's MSVC targets link with `link.exe`; a bare Windows install has
+  no C++ linker until the VS toolset is present. Today that prerequisite is **implicit**
+  even on x86-64: the guide's Prerequisites table lists only Rust, git, Node, and
+  PowerShell. On the authoring host the implicit dependency is visible — VS 2022
+  BuildTools 14.44.35207 is installed, and the repository's own transcripts quote a
+  `linking with link.exe failed` diagnosis (`docs/acceptance/gate-red-classification-2026-09-18.md`).
+  On a brand-new Snapdragon laptop that silence is precisely the "undocumented detour"
+  SC-001 forbids, so the ARM64 section writes it down.
+- **Alternatives considered**:
+  - *Document the GNU target (`aarch64-pc-windows-gnu`)* — rejected: requires a mingw
+    arm64 toolchain named nowhere in the repository and diverges from rustup's default
+    MSVC target, so it would create a second build route to maintain.
+  - *Leave MSVC implicit and learn from failures* — rejected: contradicts the
+    "expected values" discipline the rest of the guide uses (every block states what
+    the reader should see).
+
+## R3 — Proving "genuine ARM64 host, native binary": redundant signals
+
+- **Decision**: Establish both the host architecture and the binary architecture from
+  several independent signals; agreement of the set — not any single query — is the
+  acceptance bar:
+  - **Host**: `PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE` (expect `ARM64`), `Win32_ComputerSystem.SystemType`
+    (expect `ARM64-based PC`), the rustup host triple (`rustup show`, expect
+    `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc`), and the CPU model string (expect a Snapdragon/ARM part,
+    not an Intel/AMD x86 part).
+  - **Binary**: the PE COFF machine field of the installed `graphhelm.exe`, expect
+    `0xAA64` (ARM64). An x86-64 image reads `0x8664`.
+- **Rationale**: A single authoritative-looking query already proved unreliable on this
+  very host: `Win32_Processor.Architecture` returned **9 (ARM64)** on an AMD Ryzen 9
+  9950X that every other signal marks as x86-64 (`PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE=AMD64`,
+  `SystemType=x64-based PC`, rust host triple `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`, PE machine
+  `0x8664`). The spec's "Emulation ambiguity" edge case requires the record to show the
+  host is genuinely ARM64 *and* the binary is native; the Windows-on-x86 Prism
+  emulation layer is transparent to a running process, so the binary's own PE machine
+  field is the deciding signal for the executable.
+- **Alternatives considered**:
+  - *Trust `Win32_Processor.Architecture`* — rejected: measured false positive on the
+    authoring host (above).
+  - *Compare performance against an x86 baseline to detect emulation* — rejected:
+    indirect, unrepeatable, and not an observable contract.
+
+## R4 — Form and location of the verification evidence
+
+- **Decision**: One new dated Markdown transcript under `docs/acceptance/`
+  (filename `windows-arm64-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`, date chosen at verification time), modeled
+  on `docs/acceptance/install-rehearsal-2026-09-13.md`: a header table (commit under
+  test, host with OS/build/architecture, source, started/ended, result, secrets,
+  cost), a "what was verified, and what was not" section, then the transcript with each
+  command and its verbatim output. `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md` links to it exactly
+  the way it links the two existing clean-host records.
+- **Rationale**: FR-006 asks for evidence "in the same spirit as the guide's other
+  platform transcripts"; the two records the guide already cites fix the de-facto
+  format, and the "Secrets" row convention (tokens/keys as SHA-256 digests only)
+  satisfies the repository's secrets rule for records. Keeping one convention in one
+  directory makes SC-004 auditable at a glance.
+- **Alternatives considered**:
+  - *Machine-readable artifact under `target/`* — rejected: not durable, not diffable,
+    not citable from documentation.
+  - *New `docs/platforms/` tree* — rejected: `docs/acceptance/` is the established home
+    for executed-transcript records (fifteen-plus files).
+
+## R5 — `--version` output: shape accepted, number not pinned
+
+- **Decision**: Acceptance is on the **shape** — exit code 0, exactly one stdout line
+  matching `graphhelm <version>` (semver-shaped), nothing on stderr — plus the native
+  ARM64 machine check (R3). The transcript records the exact printed value *and* the
+  commit/`VERSION` state it came from. During implementation the guide's "Expected last
+  line" block (currently `graphhelm 0.1.0`) is reconciled against whatever the verified
+  build actually prints.
+- **Rationale**: Three sources disagree today: workspace `Cargo.toml` version is
+  `0.1.0`, the repository `VERSION` file reads `0.1.1-spec`, and the preinstalled
+  authoring-host binary prints `graphhelm 0.1.1` (exit 0, measured this run). The CLI
+  derives its version from the package version at build time (clap `version` attribute
+  in `apps/cli/src/args.rs`), so a source build of this checkout prints `0.1.0` while an
+  older installed preview prints `0.1.1`. Pinning either literal would make a correct
+  build fail on the next release bump; the spec's "Version drift" edge case mandates
+  shape-based acceptance.
+- **Alternatives considered**:
+  - *Pin `0.1.1`* — rejected: contradicts a fresh source build of this checkout.
+  - *Pin `0.1.0`* — rejected: stale on the next bump, and wrong against the currently
+    installed preview.
+
+## R6 — Observer status at planning time: this checkout + this machine
+
+- **Decision**: As of 2026-10-26, the core runtime promise (FR-001–FR-003, FR-006) is
+  **`OBSERVER_MISSING` on this machine**, and the design carries that as a first-class
+  branch rather than assuming a device away:
+  - **(a) Device available**: run quickstart Scenario 1 on the ARM64 laptop; the
+    transcript lands with Result **pass**; `GETTING_STARTED.md` lists Windows 11 ARM64
+    as verified (FR-004) with the exact steps and the transcript link.
+  - **(b) No device available**: the guide documents the exact ARM64 steps with an
+    honest status line ("not yet verified — missing observer, see record"), the
+    transcript is still written and names the missing observer, the platform is NOT
+    represented as verified (FR-007, US3 scenario 2), and the report ends with
+    `OBSERVER_MISSING` rather than "verified".
+- **Rationale**: The measured facts that fix this branch choice are in the appendix:
+  the host is x86-64 by four independent signals; the installed Rust toolchain carries
+  no `aarch64*` target; VS BuildTools here ships only the x64/x86 host toolsets.
+  Windows on x86-64 does not emulate ARM64 at all, so no ARM64 image can even execute
+  on this machine — an emulator or a cross-build would be a proxy, and the repository's
+  JPD rules forbid counting a proxy as proof. The spec's own edge case ("the
+  confirmation is blocked, not assumed") makes branch (b) normative, not pessimistic.
+- **Alternatives considered**:
+  - *Claim ARM64 verified on the strength of the x86-64 run* — rejected: US1 scenario 3
+    exists to say exactly that that outcome does not satisfy the ARM64 confirmation.
+  - *Ship nothing until a device appears* — rejected: SC-003 wants the steps visible to
+    readers either way, and FR-007 wants the missing observer recorded, not the task
+    dropped.
+
+## R7 — Governance reference substitution
+
+- **Decision**: No constitution file exists in this checkout. The repository-root
+  `AGENTS.md` is used as the governance reference for the plan's Constitution Check,
+  and the substitution is recorded in `plan.md` (and here) per the run's stage setup.
+- **Rationale**: Stage setup directs the substitution; `AGENTS.md` is this repository's
+  checked-in governance document (scope, JPD rules, Keel, security, delivery), so the
+  gate reads substance, not a placeholder.
+- **Alternatives considered**: *Fail the plan for the missing constitution* — rejected:
+  the stage setup explicitly anticipates the absence and names the substitute.
+
+## R8 — Scope guard: zero planned code changes; defect escalation path
+
+- **Decision**: This feature plans **documentation and acceptance-record changes only**:
+  edits to `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`, verification-only of
+  `docs/releases/0.1.0.md` (edit only if a contradiction is found), and the new
+  transcript. No crate, module, type, dependency, or `Cargo.lock` entry changes. If the
+  on-device run uncovers a genuine code or dependency defect that blocks the ARM64
+  build/run, that defect is fixed through the repository's ordinary issue-first flow
+  (its own label, branch, review, merge) and the transcript records the failure plus a
+  pointer to the fixing issue — it does not ride along in the documentation PR.
+- **Rationale**: Keel proportionality reads risk from what a change touches, not how big
+  it is; a code change mixed into a docs change gets reviewed as a docs change. The
+  spec's Out of Scope section bounds the feature to the confirmation and the guide.
+- **Alternatives considered**: *Fix discovered defects inline* — rejected: muddles
+  review attribution and violates the issue-first rule for non-trivial changes.
+
+## Appendix — Measurements (authoring host, 2026-10-26)
+
+Everything below was read on the machine running this planning session (Gigabyte
+X670 AORUS ELITE AX), to anchor R3, R5, R6, and R8.
+
+| Signal | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE` | `AMD64` |
+| `Win32_ComputerSystem.SystemType` | `x64-based PC` |
+| `Win32_Processor.Name` | AMD Ryzen 9 9950X 16-Core Processor |
+| `Win32_Processor.Architecture` | `9` (ARM64 code — conflicts with every other signal; treated as noise per R3) |
+| OS | Microsoft Windows 11 Pro, build 10.0.26200 |
+| Rust host triple (`rustup show`) | `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`; toolchain 1.97.1 active via `rust-toolchain.toml` |
+| Installed Rust targets | `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`, `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` (no `aarch64*`) |
+| VS 2022 BuildTools MSVC toolsets | 14.44.35207, host dirs `HostX64\{x64,x86}` only — no ARM64 cross tools; no `clang` on PATH |
+| Installed `graphhelm --version` | printed `graphhelm 0.1.1`, exit code 0, single line |
+| Installed `graphhelm.exe` PE machine | `0x8664` (x86-64) |
+| Workspace `Cargo.toml` version | `0.1.0` |
+| Repository `VERSION` file | `0.1.1-spec` |
+| Release archives (`docs/releases/0.1.0.md`) | `windows-x86_64.zip`, `linux-x86_64.tar.gz`, `SHA256SUMS.txt` — no ARM entry |
+| Existing clean-host records | `docs/acceptance/install-rehearsal-2026-09-13.md`, `docs/acceptance/clean-machine-2026-09-14.md` (format reference for R4) |
+
+**Conclusion**: this checkout + machine cannot observe the ARM64 runtime promise; the
+plan therefore designs both branches (R6) instead of pretending the observer exists.
