+# Feature Specification: Windows 11 ARM64 CLI Install Verification
+
+**Feature Branch**: `[001-bench]`
+
+**Created**: 2026-10-26
+
+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "Confirm that the `graphhelm` CLI installs and runs `graphhelm --version` on Windows 11 on ARM64 (a Snapdragon laptop), and add Windows ARM64 to the verified platforms in `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Developer installs and runs GraphHelm on a Snapdragon laptop (Priority: P1)
+
+A developer gets a new Windows 11 laptop on ARM64 (a Snapdragon-based device) and wants to use GraphHelm. They open the getting-started guide, follow its install steps, and want confidence the tool actually works on this hardware before investing in the rest of the setup. By the end of the install section they run `graphhelm --version` and get a real answer from a binary built for their machine.
+
+**Why this priority**: Without a confirmed-working path on ARM64, a developer on a Snapdragon laptop is left guessing whether GraphHelm runs at all, or silently ends up with an emulated/wrong-architecture build. Confirming install-and-run is the smallest slice that delivers real value: a usable CLI on the device.
+
+**Independent Test**: On a clean Windows 11 ARM64 laptop with the documented prerequisites present, run the install steps shown in the getting-started guide and then run `graphhelm --version`. Success is a clean, single-line version result with no errors, produced by a binary that matches the host architecture.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a Windows 11 ARM64 (Snapdragon) host with the prerequisites listed in the guide, **When** the developer follows the documented install steps and runs `graphhelm --version`, **Then** the command finishes successfully (exit code 0) and prints exactly one line shaped `graphhelm <version>`, with no error output.
+2. **Given** the same host after installation, **When** the installed `graphhelm` command is inspected for the machine architecture of the executable it runs, **Then** it is a native ARM64 build matching the host, not an emulated x86-64 build.
+3. **Given** a developer on an x86-64 Windows machine, **When** they run `graphhelm --version`, **Then** that outcome is recognized as NOT satisfying the ARM64 confirmation (this check is specific to an ARM64 host).
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - The guide names Windows 11 ARM64 as a verified platform (Priority: P2)
+
+A reader is deciding whether GraphHelm fits their hardware. They read the getting-started page and want it to state plainly that Windows 11 on ARM64 is a supported, verified platform, and to show the exact steps to reach a working install there — before they spend time installing.
+
+**Why this priority**: Documentation that does not name the platform forces every ARM64 user to verify the tool themselves; naming it with the precise steps turns an unknown into a known, and it is the second half of the requested change.
+
+**Independent Test**: Open `docs/install/GETTING_STARTED.md`, find the platform/verification statement, and confirm Windows 11 ARM64 appears as a verified platform with the concrete steps to reach a working `graphhelm --version`.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the getting-started page, **When** a reader looks for which platforms are verified, **Then** Windows 11 ARM64 is listed alongside the previously verified platforms.
+2. **Given** the page presents ARM64 as verified, **When** a reader compares it with the release downloads listing, **Then** the two agree and neither claims a prebuilt ARM64 download that does not exist.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - The ARM64 verification is reproducible and evidenced (Priority: P3)
+
+A maintainer later wants to know whether the "verified on ARM64" claim still holds after dependencies change. The verification should leave a recorded, repeatable proof — the same way the guide evidences its other platforms — so the claim can be trusted and re-checked.
+
+**Why this priority**: Evidence is what separates a durable "verified" label from a hopeful one; it keeps the platform claim honest over time.
+
+**Independent Test**: Re-run the documented ARM64 install-and-`--version` sequence on an equivalent Windows 11 ARM64 host and confirm the same successful result is reproduced, with the host's architecture identifiable in the record.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a recorded ARM64 verification, **When** the documented sequence is re-run on an equivalent Windows 11 ARM64 host, **Then** the same successful `graphhelm --version` outcome is reproduced.
+2. **Given** no genuine Windows 11 ARM64 device is available, **When** someone attempts to mark the platform verified, **Then** the missing observer is recorded and the platform is NOT represented as verified.
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- **No prebuilt ARM64 archive exists in the current release.** The verified ARM64 path is the documented build-from-source route. The guide must not imply a downloadable ARM64 archive exists unless and until one is published.
+- **The test/authoring host is x86-64, not ARM64.** Running `graphhelm` on an x86-64 machine (even a fast one) does not count as the ARM64 confirmation; the produced binary's architecture must match an ARM64 host.
+- **Emulation ambiguity.** Native ARM64 execution must be distinguishable from running an x86-64 binary under emulation; the verification record must show the host is genuinely ARM64 and the binary it runs is native.
+- **No ARM64 device available at verification time.** The confirmation is blocked, not assumed: the missing observer is recorded and the platform stays unverified rather than being claimed.
+- **Version drift.** The version printed by `graphhelm --version` tracks the current release and may differ from the specific preview named in older release notes; the acceptance is on the output shape, and the guide's "expected output" should reflect the live release.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: The system MUST be installable on a Windows 11 ARM64 (Snapdragon) host through the documented install path, yielding a runnable `graphhelm` command.
+- **FR-002**: On a correctly installed Windows 11 ARM64 host, `graphhelm --version` MUST exit successfully (exit code 0) and print exactly one line of the form `graphhelm <version>` with no error output.
+- **FR-003**: The `graphhelm` executable produced for the ARM64 host MUST be a native ARM64 image matching the host architecture, not an emulated foreign-architecture binary.
+- **FR-004**: The getting-started documentation MUST list Windows 11 ARM64 among verified platforms and MUST show the precise steps a developer on that platform follows to reach a working install.
+- **FR-005**: The verified-platforms statement MUST be consistent with the release downloads listing and MUST NOT assert that a prebuilt ARM64 archive exists unless one is actually published.
+- **FR-006**: The ARM64 verification MUST leave recorded, reproducible evidence showing the successful `--version` run together with the host's architecture, in the same spirit as the guide's other platform transcripts.
+- **FR-007**: When no genuine Windows 11 ARM64 device is available to confirm, the platform MUST NOT be presented as verified and the missing observer MUST be recorded instead.
+
+### Out of Scope
+
+- Publishing a brand-new prebuilt ARM64 release archive (packaging, checksums, download page): the verified path for this change is build-from-source; a prebuilt archive is a potential follow-up.
+- Studio, Runtime, or model-provider behavior beyond what `graphhelm --version` exercises on the ARM64 host.
+- Changes to other platforms' verified status, or to the existing x86-64 / Ubuntu flow.
+
+### Key Entities *(included because the feature produces a verifiable platform record)*
+
+- **Verified Platform Record**: identifies a platform (operating system + CPU architecture), the install method that was actually verified (prebuilt download vs build-from-source), its verified / not-verified status, and a pointer to the supporting evidence.
+- **Verification Evidence**: a captured, re-runnable record of the install-and-version sequence on a specific host, including the host's identifying architecture and the resulting version line, so the "verified" claim can be audited and reproduced.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: A developer on a Windows 11 ARM64 laptop, starting from only the documented prerequisites, reaches a working `graphhelm --version` in a single guided pass — zero undocumented detours required.
+- **SC-002**: On the verified ARM64 host, `graphhelm --version` succeeds on 100% of invocations (deterministic clean exit, one-line version output).
+- **SC-003**: 100% of getting-started readers can identify, before installing, that Windows 11 ARM64 is a verified platform and can see the exact steps to a working install.
+- **SC-004**: The getting-started platform statement and the release downloads listing share 0 contradictions (in particular, no claimed prebuilt ARM64 download that does not exist).
+- **SC-005**: The ARM64 verification is reproducible: at least one transcript is retained, and re-running the documented sequence on an equivalent Windows 11 ARM64 host reproduces the same successful outcome with 0 unexpected failures.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- **Target device**: "Windows 11 on ARM64" means a genuine ARM64 Windows 11 device (for example a Snapdragon-based laptop). Confirming the claim requires such a device to be present when the verification runs.
+- **Install path is build-from-source.** The current preview release publishes only Windows x86-64 and Linux x86-64 archives, so for this change the verified ARM64 path is the guide's existing build-from-source route. Introducing a prebuilt ARM64 archive is out of scope (see Out of Scope).
+- **"Verified" follows the repository's existing standard**: the documented commands were actually executed on that platform and the transcript retained, mirroring how the guide evidences its Windows x86-64 and Ubuntu platforms.
+- **Authoring-host observation (recorded 2026-10-26)**: this specification was authored on a Windows 11 Pro (build 26200) machine whose processor is an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (x86-64). A `Win32_Processor.Architecture` query returned the ARM64 code (9), but that single reading conflicts with `PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE=AMD64`, `SystemType=x64-based PC`, the Rust host triple `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`, and a preinstalled x86-64 `graphhelm.exe` (PE machine `0x8664`). The authoring host is therefore treated as **x86-64** and is *not* evidence for the ARM64 confirmation. On that host `graphhelm --version` was observed to exit 0 and print `graphhelm 0.1.1`, which anchors the expected single-line output shape.
+- **Version not pinned.** Acceptance is on the shape `graphhelm <version>`, not a fixed number; the live binary currently reports `0.1.1`, while the 0.1.0 release notes still quote `0.1.0`. During implementation the guide's "expected output" should be reconciled with the current release.
+- **English-only documentation** per the repository rule for all documentation.
+- **Governance reference substitution**: no project constitution file exists in this checkout; per the run's stage setup, the repository-root `AGENTS.md` is used as the governance reference in place of a constitution document.
